##
    Mistakes about what a function remembers.

    This program does not compile. Each line below is a way of misreading what a kept
    function names — and each one, left alone, would be quiet rather than wrong in a way
    anything shows.

    samples/closures.pc is the same ground written correctly.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.MovingACounter();
        Program.HidingWhatIsKept();
        Program.ReachingForAnInstanceThatIsNotThere();
        Program.CallingBeforeWhatItUsesIsReady();
    end function

    ##
        A loop counter cannot be assigned to.

        Each turn binds a fresh one, which is what lets a function made on one turn keep that
        turn's value. So a write here would change that turn's counter and nothing else — the
        loop would go on as before, and the line would look like it did something.
    ##
    function MovingACounter()
        for i = 1 to 3
            i = i + 1;
        end for
    end function

    ##
        A name inside a kept function cannot hide one around it.

        Reading such a body means asking, of every name, whether it belongs to the function or
        to the code around it. Where the two spellings are the same there is nothing in the
        line to tell you, so the second is refused rather than shadowing the first.
    ##
    function HidingWhatIsKept()
        integer total = 1;

        delegate() report = function()
            integer total = 2;
            Console.WriteLine(total);
        end function;

        report();
    end function

    ##
        A kept function may name 'this' only where 'this' means something.

        A shared member runs without an instance, so there is none for a function written
        inside it to keep. The refusal is the same one a shared member gets for naming 'this'
        directly: being inside a function value changes nothing about it.
    ##
    function ReachingForAnInstanceThatIsNotThere()
        integer delegate() reader = () yield this.missing;

        Console.WriteLine(reader());
    end function

    ##
        A function declared among statements may be called from above its own line — but not
        from above a name it uses.

        Being in scope throughout the run is about where the name may be written. The locals
        it reaches for still come into being in order, so a call up here would read a place
        holding nothing yet, and answer with it rather than say so.
    ##
    function CallingBeforeWhatItUsesIsReady()
        Console.WriteLine(Doubled());

        integer total = 7;

        integer function Doubled()
            yield total * 2;
        end function
    end function

end model
